plants aren't always operating at 100%  efficiency in fact in great sunlight  they ramp down the efficiency of  photosynthesis to avoid damage to key  molecules the drop-off is abrupt the  plants need to protect themselves but  getting back up to speed is slow like  the way your eyes slowly adjust after  entering a dark room  it's estimated that 20% of plant  productivity is lost to the slow ramp-up  in response to rapid changes in light  that's a lot of corn not made where does  all this variation and light come from  anyway from clouds passing over the Sun  from wind blowing leaves around casting  and moving shadows from other plants  even their own leaves in fact from the  point of view of a leaf even the change  in the angle of light as the Sun moves  through the sky can cause abrupt  variations researchers wanted to see if  they change this curve the speed at  which a plant returns to full efficiency  if that could have a big impact on  productivity by swapping a few genes  from one plant to another they were able  to change photosynthetic recovery-time  plants with these genetic changes  recovered about 15% of their  productivity giving them bigger broader  leaves taller stocks even more heft in  general since the mechanisms targeted in  this research are common to a large  swath of plants this could be tested in  all kinds of crops for soybean breeder  is a 2 percent increase in yield is huge  so what would a 15 percent increase in  yield give us use less land for  agriculture feed more people maybe both
 

